WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:09.049
The vast environments of the video game ‘Monster
Hunter: World’ are home to incredible forms

00:00:09.049 --> 00:00:14.360
of life — from thundering behemoths… to
ravenous hunters.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:19.980
While the game focuses on battling monsters,
every creature on this continent has a life

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:26.950
of their own, with unique day-to-day patterns,
biological intricacies, and roles in the larger

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:27.950
ecosystems.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:33.480
So, for this entry into the Archive, we’ll
show the life of this continent in entirely

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.210
new ways, and bring you closer to these creatures
than ever before.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:43.540
And although doing fieldwork in this region
represents a significant risk, I’m determined

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.510
to document these extreme lifeforms firsthand.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:57.360
So, let’s embark on this expedition together,
and discover what awaits in a land of giants…

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.520
On the southern shores of this enormous continent,
one of the first human settlements lies in

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.299
the shadow of the surrounding wilderness.

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:10.581
The village of Astera is walled off from the
dangers of the region’s interior, and will

00:01:10.581 --> 00:01:14.070
serve as a useful base of operations for this
voyage.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:19.920
I’m staying in an accommodation that my
host assures me is in no way a hastily converted

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.240
storage room.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:26.360
The ceiling doesn’t seem… particularly
waterproof, but even in these humble lodgings,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.210
I can smell the promise of discovery.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.490
Or, perhaps that’s the smell of dirty laundry…

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:37.850
In either case, with the sun just beginning
to rise, I check my supplies one last time

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:44.820
and set off towards the wilderness beyond
the safety of Astera’s walls…

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.700
To the west, the most impressive forest on
the continent awaits.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:56.590
This age-old environment is aptly called the
Ancient Forest, and it is a garden of wonders.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:02.369
From sunlit clearings to dizzying heights,
each region of the Ancient Forest is an ecosystem

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:04.520
unto itself.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:10.129
Beginning in a clearing along the shoreline,
I find a herd of slow-moving herbivores basking

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:12.049
in the early-morning sun.

00:02:12.049 --> 00:02:17.590
These are Aptonoths, and despite their size,
they are among the gentlest species on the

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:18.590
continent.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:24.430
They spend their days grazing on plentiful
vegetation and enjoying long sips of water

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.170
from the streams that flow through this region.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:33.329
Indeed, I find Aptonoths are so mild mannered,
I can walk among them in safety.

00:02:33.329 --> 00:02:39.319
Close observations reveal the Aptonoths live
in multi-family units, with infant Aptonoths

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:43.450
never straying too far from the watchful eye
of a parent.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.769
For the Ancient Forest conceals many dangers.

00:02:46.769 --> 00:02:51.590
And while Aptonoths have evolved an impressive
spiked club at the end of their tails for

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:56.610
defense, there are creatures in this environment
that might consider even the mightiest Aptonoth

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.980
nothing more than an appetizer.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:05.189
To find such lifeforms, I’ll have to venture
deeper into the forest…

00:03:05.189 --> 00:03:10.639
At the edge of the Ancient Forest, the vegetation
becomes thicker, and the branches cast long

00:03:10.639 --> 00:03:12.439
shadows over the undergrowth.

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:17.319
And slinking among the foliage is a stealthy
social predator.

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:23.230
These are Jagras — nimble, reptilian lifeforms
whose mottled green and yellow coloration

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.809
helps them blend into their environment.

00:03:25.809 --> 00:03:30.839
Relatively small by the standards of this
continent, a single Jagras couldn’t hold

00:03:30.839 --> 00:03:33.599
their own against most of the creatures that
live here.

00:03:33.599 --> 00:03:38.569
But like a wolf pack, the Jagras have solved
this problem by sticking together.

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:44.220
They travel together, hunt together, and when
one of them gets a kill… they eat together.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.439
While they hunt in groups of two to three,
any spoils are split amongst all members of

00:03:49.439 --> 00:03:50.439
the pack.

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:54.079
And with so many mouths, the meals rarely
last...

00:03:54.079 --> 00:03:59.949
When all scraps have disappeared, the different
units silently split off once more, continuing

00:03:59.949 --> 00:04:02.769
their persistent search for unwary prey…

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:06.349
And Jagras don’t just hunt on the forest
floor.

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:10.829
Their sinewy bodies are perfect for prowling
across the branches above.

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:15.949
In a forest whose branches form a network
of natural causeways, an attack can truly

00:04:15.949 --> 00:04:17.139
come from anywhere.

00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:22.120
I’ll have to stay on my guard as I venture
deeper into Jagras territory…

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:28.410
Peeking out from the underbrush, I glimpse
the entrance to a cave that the Jagras use

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:29.930
as a nest.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:33.190
And from this opening, a titan emerges.

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:35.060
This is a Great Jagras.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:40.620
A well-muscled, several-ton behemoth that
lumbers through the forest in search of food.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:44.850
While I at first assumed the Great Jagras
to be a separate species from the smaller

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:49.420
Jagras, they’re actually a highly specialized
member of the pack.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:54.920
Like a Silverback Gorilla, the Great Jagras
is bigger and heavier than the other group

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:55.920
members.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.270
And they have to be, for the Great Jagras
don’t feed on small forest life like their

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:01.940
Jagras packmates.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.680
They feed on Aptonoths.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:05.680
Adult Aptonoths.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:12.010
And to take down creatures as large as Aptonoths,
the Great Jagras will need to be quick and

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:13.010
decisive.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:18.240
Once they catch the scent of their quarry,
the Great Jagras is off, charging with surprising

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:19.240
speed.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:25.520
In an instant, the chase is over, and the
Great Jagras swallows their prey… whole.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:31.380
This alarming display might seem truly monstrous,
but we can find similar behavior in various

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:32.820
Earth animals.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.470
Crocodile jaws aren't wired to move sideways,
so they can’t chew, and must instead swallow

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:40.310
chunks of food whole.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:45.120
The same is true for certain snakes, which
have evolved to gulp down prey larger than

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.290
their skulls thanks to a stretchy elastic
ligament in their lower jawbone.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:55.110
We can assume the Great Jagras have converged
on a similar jaw structure.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:59.360
Once a Great Jagras has swallowed their prey,
they need to make a quick exit.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.930
With a full gullet weighing them down, they’re
vulnerable to predation, and move with caution.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:09.990
But eventually, this Great Jagras drags their
way back to the safety of the nest.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:14.740
Here, the Great Jagras heaves up a portion
of their meal and lets out a roar.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:15.740
Dinner is served.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:21.450
On cue, the other Jagras rush in and consume
the leftovers their larger packmate has left

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.450
them.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:26.140
This extra nutrition will help the smaller
Jagras survive.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:32.180
This dynamic is certainly a bit disgusting,
but far from unheard of in the animal kingdom.

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:36.310
Various types of birds bring food back for
their young in a similar manner.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:41.400
And while the Great Jagras isn’t the parent
of the Jagras, they do assume a kind of parental

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:47.020
role within the pack — providing food and
protecting the nest against various outside

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.250
threats.

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:54.070
But there is one lifeform that can slip past
the Great Jagras unseen.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:07:00.110
The Kulu-Ya-Ku is a bird-like species that
is remarkably crafty, and knows all the ins

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.950
and outs of the Ancient Forest.

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:08.470
And most notably, the Kulu-Ya-Ku has memorized
when the Great Jagras leave the nest to hunt,

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:13.360
which helps the Kulu-Ya-Ku score their favorite
food — Jagras eggs.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.810
On Earth, many generalist predators consume
the eggs of ground-nesting birds to supplement

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:19.810
their diet.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:25.370
But the Kulu-Ya-Ku isn’t stealing bird eggs,
but rather the eggs of a lifeform many times

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:26.370
their size.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:29.760
So, this bird brain will need to be careful.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.870
Sneaking into Jagras territory, the Kulu-Ya-Ku
pauses at the nest’s entrance.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:39.020
They know that if they’re caught in the
act, they’ll be in serious trouble.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:41.760
But fortune favors the bold.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.389
Descending into the cave, their prize awaits.

00:07:44.389 --> 00:07:47.580
Egg in hand, there’s no point in hesitating.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.480
Running is now the best strategy.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:53.530
The further they can get from the nest, the
better…

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.210
Safe at last.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:59.830
Holding the egg in their long forelimbs, the
Kulu-Ya-Ku digs in.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:04.180
This heist was successful, but the next one
might not be.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:07.870
But the Kulu-Ya-Ku isn’t as helpless as
they might appear.

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:13.100
When threatened, the Kulu-Ya-Ku will pick
up a rock and use it to fend off other lifeforms.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:18.180
I learned about this behavior when I followed
one too closely, and they tried to bash me

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:19.770
over the head.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:25.669
Fortunately, the Kulu-Ya-Ku is rather cowardly,
and once I stood my ground, they turned away.

00:08:25.669 --> 00:08:30.830
Tailing them more cautiously, I noticed the
Kulu-Ya-Ku will also use their flexible digits

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:37.199
to scratch in a very human-like manner, leaving
brilliant feathers on the forest floor.

00:08:37.199 --> 00:08:42.219
The Kulu-Ya-Ku might be on the small side
for this environment, but with a keen intellect,

00:08:42.219 --> 00:08:46.029
they’re able to eke out a life here…

00:08:46.029 --> 00:08:49.649
Yet every environment needs an apex predator.

00:08:49.649 --> 00:08:55.720
For the lower reaches of the Ancient Forest,
that predator is the Anjanath.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.350
A huge, bipedal hunter, the Anjanath can feed
on just about anything that walks upon the

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:01.350
ground.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:07.170
Even a Great Jagras is hopelessly outmatched
against the terror that is the Anjanath.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:11.170
But the Anjanath didn’t reach the top just
by being the biggest.

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:16.249
To maintain their status within the lower
forest food web, this species has evolved

00:09:16.249 --> 00:09:20.050
an array of unexpected adaptations.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:26.290
The Anjanath’s nose, for example, is a strange
fleshy appendage on the tip of their snout

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:31.889
that expands when tracking prey, and greatly
enhances the creature’s sense of smell.

00:09:31.889 --> 00:09:36.500
Like the nose of a bear, the Anjanath’s
snout contains hundreds of times more scent

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.430
receptors than a human’s.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:43.370
It’s… an odd-looking sniffer, to be sure,
but a highly effective one.

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:49.089
For the Anjanath, having such a large nose
also means a frequent buildup of mucus, which

00:09:49.089 --> 00:09:53.029
the Anjanath habitually sneezes out to mark
their territory.

00:09:53.029 --> 00:09:59.070
It’s certainly a gross strategy — although
the pungent mixture does send an unmistakable

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.269
signal to others that this district is taken.

00:10:02.269 --> 00:10:06.879
And this isn’t the only bewildering adaptation
of the Anjanath.

00:10:06.879 --> 00:10:12.689
When charging into battle, the Anjanath extends
two massive sails from their back, which help

00:10:12.689 --> 00:10:15.170
the Anjanath appear even larger.

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:17.850
And these sails have another function.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:24.399
In a tropical climate like this one, a creature
as large as an Anjanath is in danger of overheating.

00:10:24.399 --> 00:10:30.560
By extending these sails, the Anjanath can
radiate heat outwards while lying in the shade.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:36.759
Certain lizards, like Igaunas, have similar
sails under their neck that help with thermoregulation.

00:10:36.759 --> 00:10:41.311
The Anjanath might be formidable, but like
all creatures in the Ancient Forest, they

00:10:41.311 --> 00:10:44.449
are more than just a monster…

00:10:44.449 --> 00:10:49.500
Further to the north, the forest gives way
to a shimmering seascape.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:54.220
Along this coast, a herd of Kestodon stretch
their legs.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:11:00.249
Like the Aptonoths, the Kestodons are herbivores,
and have evolved numerous defenses to survive

00:11:00.249 --> 00:11:04.220
in this ecosystem of teeth and claws.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:07.630
Kestodon skulls possess an extremely thick
roof.

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:12.690
This allows them to charge potential threats,
without fear of injuring their skulls — a

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:16.939
behavior I observed first hand when I crept
too close.

00:11:16.939 --> 00:11:22.499
From a safe distance, however, the Kestodon
herd appears remarkably peaceful.

00:11:22.499 --> 00:11:28.980
Curiously, male Kestodon skull plates are
more extravagant, likely to aid in intra-species

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:29.980
combat.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:34.819
Battles for dominance are a familiar sight
on Earth, with numerous types of animals competing

00:11:34.819 --> 00:11:40.129
with other members of their own species — often
at the risk of their own life.

00:11:40.129 --> 00:11:45.319
But based on my limited observations, Kestodon
herds seem more diplomatic.

00:11:45.319 --> 00:11:50.300
In a biosphere of vicious competition, it’s
nice to see a species that’s a bit more

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.160
group-oriented.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.230
Other species have less pleasant dispositions.

00:11:56.230 --> 00:12:01.629
In the spore-filled region at the northeastern
edge of the forest, an ill-tempered omnivore

00:12:01.629 --> 00:12:03.720
patrols their hunting path.

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:09.670
This brightly colored creature is a Pukei-Pukei,
and while they are only a medium sized organism,

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:13.700
they have a giant trick up their sleeve — or
rather, in their mouth.

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:19.220
The Pukei-Pukei’s tongue is nearly as long
as the creature themselves, and can be used

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:21.759
like a whip to stun other lifeforms.

00:12:21.759 --> 00:12:27.759
The equally chromatic chameleons have a similar
tongue-to-body ratio, and like the Pukei-Pukei,

00:12:27.759 --> 00:12:30.860
can lash out with their tongues with blazing
speed.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:34.259
But the Pukei-Pukei is more than a one-trick
lifeform.

00:12:34.259 --> 00:12:40.230
During one of my observation sessions, one
specimen launched a glob of a toxic compound

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:42.010
quite near my head.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:47.189
As it turns out, the Pukei-Pukei is a highly
venomous species, thanks in part to their

00:12:47.189 --> 00:12:48.559
unique diet.

00:12:48.559 --> 00:12:53.480
The Pukei-Pukei feeds primarily on the toxic
seeds of the plants that flourish in this

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:59.459
region of the forest, and can turn their food
into a deadly mixture within their stomachs.

00:12:59.459 --> 00:13:05.220
On Earth, Cinnabar Moths are known to ingest
poisonous plants and convert them into toxins

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:07.550
of their own within their own bodies.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:13.689
The Pukei-Pukei don’t just spit liquid venom,
however, but can also release a foul gas cloud

00:13:13.689 --> 00:13:16.290
that is deadly to breathe.

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:22.019
Despite this harrowing display of power, to
my surprise, the tiny terror soon retreats.

00:13:22.019 --> 00:13:27.709
Further observations show that the Pukei-Pukei
is the bully of the Ancient Forest, terrorizing

00:13:27.709 --> 00:13:32.499
smaller creatures but flying away in the face
of anything that seems threatening.

00:13:32.499 --> 00:13:36.930
Not such a scary lifeform after all…

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:42.489
All sorts of smaller creatures inhabit the
thick and lush greenery of the lower forest,

00:13:42.489 --> 00:13:47.819
living through their own daily dramas unbeknownst
to the tree-shaking titans this region is

00:13:47.819 --> 00:13:48.910
known for.

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:53.689
Yet the role these tiny critters play in the
ecosystem shouldn’t be overlooked.

00:13:53.689 --> 00:13:57.709
And while there are too many to name, one
in particular stands out.

00:13:57.709 --> 00:13:59.110
The Mosswine.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:03.250
These delightful creatures spend their days
sniffing out their favorite food, which in

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:05.629
the Mosswine’s case happens to be mushrooms.

00:14:05.629 --> 00:14:11.860
At a glance, the snuffling Mosswine resembles
an Earth pig or wild boar, with the notable

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:16.720
addition of a bed of moss that seems to be
growing from the fur on their back.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:21.980
This coating of moss forms as a result of
the Mosswines largely inactive lifestyle,

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:25.189
and helps them blend into the green of the
underbrush.

00:14:25.189 --> 00:14:30.390
Various types of sloths can be seen with plant
matter growing on their fur for a similar

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:31.390
reason.

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:35.910
Yet unlike the sloth, the Mosswine’s role
in the ecosystem of the forest they inhabit

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:38.069
is absolutely critical.

00:14:38.069 --> 00:14:43.160
According to my research, through their natural
routines, the Mosswine keeps the population

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:49.829
of fungus at a healthy level, affecting the
balance of the entire region…

00:14:49.829 --> 00:14:55.850
Yet there is one lifeform so ravenous, they
can throw the entire forest out of balance.

00:14:55.850 --> 00:15:02.249
This heavily-scarred colossus is a Deviljho,
and they are a stranger to the Ancient Forest.

00:15:02.249 --> 00:15:08.570
The Deviljho is a nomadic hunter, known for
wandering vast distances in search of prey.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:13.529
Their status as a super-predator allows them
to overtake the territory of organisms that

00:15:13.529 --> 00:15:15.260
stand in their path.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:20.170
As a result, the Deviljho wreaks havoc on
any ecosystem they invade.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:26.319
On our own planet, invasive predators, like
Lionfish in the North Atlantic, pose a similar

00:15:26.319 --> 00:15:27.329
threat.

00:15:27.329 --> 00:15:31.600
Organisms adapted for one environment that
move to another — either by their own accord

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:37.560
or, in the Lionfish’s case, human negligence
— often find themselves in an ecosystem

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:41.899
with prey that have no defenses against their
specific hunting strategy.

00:15:41.899 --> 00:15:44.620
And the Deviljo are no different.

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:48.769
Thankfully, however, the Deviljo seem to be
few in number.

00:15:48.769 --> 00:15:54.989
Indeed, with so few Deviljo on this continent,
many seem unhealthy due to low genetic diversity.

00:15:54.989 --> 00:15:59.779
Soon, the biosphere of the forest may be free
of their influence.

00:15:59.779 --> 00:16:04.709
I’ve now explored the lower reaches of Ancient
Forest.

00:16:04.709 --> 00:16:10.300
Yet there is a new world waiting in the branches
above, home to even more extreme forms of

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:11.300
life.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:14.549
It’s a long and dizzying climb upwards.

00:16:14.549 --> 00:16:16.399
Best not to look down…

00:16:16.399 --> 00:16:21.269
While making the trek upwards, I cross paths
with a seldom-glimpsed wonder.

00:16:21.269 --> 00:16:24.429
This enigmatic traverser of the treetops is
a Tobi-Kadachi.

00:16:24.429 --> 00:16:30.649
A stealthy loner, the Tobi-Kadachi can move,
cat-like across the branches.

00:16:30.649 --> 00:16:34.079
And the Tobi-Kadachi has a secret ability.

00:16:34.079 --> 00:16:38.049
Periodically, the creature will brush up against
nearby objects.

00:16:38.049 --> 00:16:43.619
As a result, vast amounts of static electricity
collect in the Tobi-Kadachi’s specialized

00:16:43.619 --> 00:16:48.290
spines, which they can unleash upon any creature
that bothers them.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:53.089
On Earth, honeybees are known to beat their
wings at such a fast rate that they generate

00:16:53.089 --> 00:16:58.550
an electrical charge, which they use to help
them keep track of which flowers they’ve

00:16:58.550 --> 00:16:59.550
pollinated.

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:04.329
Static electricity typically isn’t strong
enough to cause much damage, however, which

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:10.850
is why hunters like the Electric Catfish generate
their shocks using specialized electric organs.

00:17:10.850 --> 00:17:16.680
And considering the paralyzing power the Tobi-Kadachi’s
zaps seem to have, I’d theorize that they

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:20.770
also have some form of internal electric organ...

00:17:20.770 --> 00:17:25.579
With this scuffle over with, the Tobi-Kadachi
retreats higher up into the canopy.

00:17:25.579 --> 00:17:31.340
With an impressive leap, they return to their
nest high in the branches, and stretch out

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:34.240
for the equivalent of an afternoon cat-nap.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:40.140
Considering the power the Tobi-Kadachi possesses,
it’s best to continue on, and not disturb

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:41.740
their slumber…

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:45.580
At long last, I reach the highest point of
the forest.

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:50.560
Here, at the crown of the canopy, a curious
environment is waiting.

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:55.980
This large clearing is defined by charred
wood and piles of discarded bones.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:18:01.250
It almost seems like a single, giant nest…
but for what?

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:03.510
From the sky comes the answer.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:09.680
This is a Rathalos, a flying lifeform and
the true ruler of the Ancient Forest.

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:15.510
The Rathalos spend their days soaring over
their domain on vast wings, occasionally touching

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:18.060
down to feed upon the lifeforms below.

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:23.660
When a Rathalos feels challenged, they will
end disputes quickly with a powerful jet of

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:25.740
internally-produced flame.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:31.110
And since any given Rathalos must maintain
a wide territory, they’ve evolved to be

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:32.880
quite aggressive towards intruders.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:36.200
I think it would be wise for me to stay out
of sight.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:42.530
Yet as the sun sets, I manage to observe another
side to these widely-feared creatures.

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:48.270
I watch this Rathalos preen their scales while
they sit atop their treetop throne, before

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:50.990
curling up and going to sleep for the night.

00:18:50.990 --> 00:18:55.950
While a Rathalos could certainly spell my
doom while awake, this moment of stillness

00:18:55.950 --> 00:19:03.480
helps demonstrate that many of these organisms
— while powerful — aren’t truly monstrous.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:09.640
All the same, I find that I can breathe easier
once I’m back in the familiar glow of civilization.

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:14.750
The village of Astera is a welcome sight after
a long and risky expedition.

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:19.910
And despite the still-present smell of old
laundry, my accommodations have never seemed

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:20.930
better...

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:25.990
Elsewhere on the continent, there are more
fascinating regions to explore and more extreme

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:30.500
lifeforms, and I’ll likely set out on another
expedition soon.

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:34.540
Let me know if you liked this style of video
and want to see more.

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:38.140
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:42.990
If you enjoyed this entry, you can lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:47.680
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:56.180
See you in the next video.

